      "translation_evidence": "The translation contains exact identities and derivative relations, without an error or stability notion.",
      "reasoning": "No error, conditioning, or stability concept is present to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "q is the exact translated profile.",
      "translation_evidence": "q is defined by exact real evaluation of f(x − ct).",
      "reasoning": "No approximation, rounding, perturbation, or computed surrogate replaces the exact field."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "Equations (1.2)–(1.3) contain no asymptotic approximation or remainder.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation states exact equalities; limits appear only in the ordinary definition of derivative.",
      "reasoning": "There is no asymptotic expansion, truncation order, or higher-order term requiring comparison."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source asserts a family of solutions without uniqueness and leaves regularity and solution meaning unspecified.",
      "translation_evidence": "The constructed field is a pointwise classical solution under global differentiability; no uniqueness or exhaustive classification is asserted.",
      "reasoning": "The family-of-solutions scope is preserved, but its regularity coverage and solution notion remain unresolved."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The translation establishes the exact formula, unconditional shape preservation, and the classical PDE assertion for differentiable profiles. It makes no solution assertion for nondifferentiable profiles. If the source's arbitrary-profile wording includes generalized solutions, that part is missing; if the source implicitly restricts its PDE assertion to classically admissible profiles, the translation captures it. The supplied source does not settle this distinction."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Equation (1.3) implies q(x + ct,t) = f(x) for all real x,t. For an everywhere differentiable profile, ordinary differentiation gives q_t = −c f'(x − ct) and q_x = f'(x − ct), hence actual derivative witnesses satisfying q_t + c q_x = 0 everywhere. This restricted classical consequence does not require resolving how the source treats nonsmooth profiles."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-profile-regularity-and-solution-scope",
      "source_evidence": "The sentence introducing equation (1.3) asserts solutions, and the following text permits any function q̃, while the earlier paragraph defines subscripts as partial derivatives without specifying a generalized solution notion.",
      "translation_evidence": "The PDE assertion is conditional on everywhere differentiability of f and requires actual partial derivative witnesses at every real pair. For other profiles, only the translation identity is asserted.",
      "impact": "Equivalence cannot be certified without adjudicating the source's intended scope. Under a broader arbitrary-profile solution interpretation, the translation omits cases; under an implicit classical differentiability interpretation, it may be equivalent. The additional antecedent cannot itself be counted as stronger."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The supplied primary-source rendering and blind translation agree on real scalar objects, global space and time, constant signed velocity, the translation formula, exact shape preservation, and the absence of uniqueness. The unresolved issue is the source's arbitrary-profile solution assertion versus the translation's explicitly conditional classical PDE conclusion. Source-to-translation implication is supported, but the reverse implication requires adjudication."
